# Git commits — agent-owned

The user does not want to decide when to commit. The agent must handle commits.

## Do commit without asking when
- A meaningful slice of work is done and working (feature, fix, UX trim, publishable milestone)
- About to start a risky or large next change
- The session would otherwise leave important uncommitted work behind

## Do not commit
- Broken mid-change states
- Secrets (`.env`, credentials, keys)
- Empty or noise-only diffs
- While still actively iterating on the same unfinished slice in the same turn

## How
- Follow the repo Git Safety Protocol (no force push, no config changes, no amend unless allowed)
- Short commit message focused on why
- Prefer one commit per completed slice, not per tiny edit

## Push — also agent-owned
The user does not want to decide when to push either. Push without asking when:
- A meaningful local commit (or set of commits) is ready and the work should be backed up on the remote
- The public demo / Pages / shareable link needs updating
- Ending a substantial work slice where leaving commits only local would risk loss

Do **not** push:
- Broken or untested mid-work
- Secrets
- Force-push, or push to rewrite shared history, unless the user explicitly ordered that

After push, say briefly that it is on the remote. Do not ask the user to click Commit or Push in the UI.
